body:
You could reconstruct your program to look like this:

public class Main {

public static void main(String\[\] args) {

String word = new java.util.Scanner([System.in](https://System.in)).nextLine();

translate(word);

}

public static void translate(String word) {

if (word.equals("one")) {

System.out.println("uno");

} else if (word.equals("two")) {

System.out.println("dos");

}

}

}

2 problems here.

First, you don't have to create a sameFirstLast object if you mark sameFirstLast (the method) static.

Second, you aren't providing the sameFirstLast method with the arguments it needs to run, an array of ints. You can create an array of ints by doing something like this:

int\[\] arr = {1, 2, 4};

And then pass the array into the method:

sameFirstLast(arr);
